<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Khiva</h1><p class="st-meta">1511 CE – 1920 CE · capital Khiva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Uzbek khanate of Khwarazm, ruled from the walled city of Khiva for four centuries, notorious for its slave markets and finally a Russian protectorate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1511 Ilbars and Balbars, descendants of Arab Shah of the Shibanid line, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> had briefly seized it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabshahid-dynasty">Arabshahid dynasty</a> ruled at first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Urgench</a> and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, which became the capital in the 17th century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> changed course.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> tribes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarts">Sarts</a> along the irrigated lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>. The khan and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-ghazi-bahadur">Abu'l-Ghazi Bahadur</a> wrote a genealogical history of the Turks. Slave trading of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and Russians captured by Turkmen raiders was central to the economy. From 1804 the Qungrat dynasty, whose founders had been inaks, ruled as khans, building much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itchan-kala">Itchan Kala</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Russian forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> in 1873 and made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. In 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-khivans">Young Khivans</a> deposed the last khan, Said Abdullah, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khorezm-peoples-soviet-republic">Khorezm People's Soviet Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-khiva.jpg" alt="Khanate of Khiva" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Khanate_of_Khiva.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1511 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khiva</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ilbars, Abu'l-Ghazi Bahadur, Muhammad Rahim Khan II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uzbek, Chagatai Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, Kazakhstan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khiva Khanate, Khwarazm Khanate, Arabshahid Khanate</dd></dl></aside></div>
